  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 149 PM MDT Sat Oct 10 2026\n\nAfternoon satellite imagery shows cloud cover increasing across\neastern Idaho as an upper-level trough continues to dig into \nthe Pacific Northwest. Well to our south, tropical moisture \nassociated with Tropical Storm Rachel is surging northward into \nCalifornia and the southern Great Basin. We could see just \nenough breaks in cloud cover this afternoon to yield marginal \ninstability, supporting an isolated thunderstorm threat later \ntoday, though the vast majority of precipitation is expected to \nfall as showers. A Flood Watch became effective earlier this \nafternoon and continues across much of eastern Idaho. The bulk \nof the precipitation will overspread the area during the \novernight hours and continue throughout the day tomorrow. \nTemperatures today are already 15 to 20 degrees cooler than 24 \nhours ago.\n\nRainfall will peak on Sunday as tropical moisture interacts \nwith the incoming trough. Forecast precipitation amounts remain \nlargely on track, with a broad swath of 1.0 to 2.0 inches \nexpected across the Magic Valley, the Snake Plain, and into the \neastern highlands. National Blend of Models (NBM) probabilities \nremain high, showing a 70% to 100% chance of exceeding 1.0 inch \nin these areas. Probabilities for exceeding 2.0 inches are \ngenerally coming in lower around 20% to 50%, though they peak at\n60% to 80% around Island Park. Before all is said and done, \nlocalized totals could certainly climb above 2.0 to 3.0 inches, \nespecially across the eastern highlands and higher terrain where\norographic lift will be maximized. Meanwhile, totals across the\ncentral mountains are currently running in the 0.33 to 0.66 \ninch range, though there is still a bit that can change.\n\nIn addition to the heavy rain, temperatures will drop another \n10 to 15 degrees as we move into Sunday. Daytime highs tomorrow \nwill only top out in the upper 40s to upper 50s across the \nregion. Despite the cold surface temperatures, snow levels will \nremain high enough to limit any frozen precipitation strictly to\nthe highest terrain and mountain peaks.\n\nPrecipitation chances will linger into the early part of the\nworkweek as the low will be slow to depart the area. Models keep\nthe low moving slowly across the Intermountain West until\nWednesday or Thursday. This will result in temperatures staying\nwell below seasonal norms, with highs mainly in the 50s until\nperhaps Thursday. By Thursday, ridging slowly builds over the\narea, finally replacing the persistent troughiness over the\nPacific Northwest and allowing conditions to moderate.",
